parent. If all children are removed, then the node becomes a leaf (no children) and we can consider removing it. So the algorithm:\n\n- Initialize set of removed node IDs empty.\n- For each node in sorted order (descending depth, then ascending ID):\n   - If node is root (parent null) or symbol in required_symbols or not removable: skip.\n   - Check if node has any children that are not in removed set. If yes, skip (because node has retained children).\n   - Tentatively assume we remove this node (and its subtree, but all children are already removed). Then check if all required symbols are still present somewhere in the tree (excluding the removed node and its subtree? Since all children are already removed, the only symbol lost is the node's own symbol). So we need to check that after removal, there exists at least one node (non-removed) for each required symbol. Since the node's symbol might be required, we already skip that. So for non-required symbols, removing it might cause a required symbol to disappear if that symbol was only present in this node? But required symbols are specific symbols, and we skip nodes with required symbols, so removing a node with a required symbol is not allowed. So the only risk is that a required symbol might be represented only by nodes that are already removed? But we only remove nodes that are not required, so required symbols remain. Actually, if a required symbol appears in multiple nodes, we could remove some of them, but we must ensure at least one remains. So we need to count occurrences of each required symbol among non-removed nodes. We should maintain counts.\n\nBetter: Maintain a counter of how many nodes with each required symbol are currently retained. Initially, count for each symbol in required_symbols from all nodes. But note: the root symbol \"root\" is required, but root is not removable anyway. So we can count all nodes initially. Then, when we tentatively remove a node, we decrement the count for its symbol. If